From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Sat, 24 Sep 1994 14:24:46 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-458] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

>From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
>X-Message-Number: #94-9-433
>
>I don't know anything about Bose-Einstein
>condensation - what role might such a coherent state play in the cell?
>The above hints at some energy-storage role, though I'm only guessing.
>What do Frolich and his recent supporters suggest?  Might there be a
>coordinating role so that the cell can affect many transmembrance proteins
>at once?  The experimental evidence is suggestive, but does it uncover
>fundamental functionality or side effects produced by unusual laboratory
>conditions?
>

These are important questions... I don't believe the answers are known.
However, the interest in this field of research is rising rapidly.  For
example, there is an entire book of recent papers titled "Rethinking Neural
Networks:  Quantum Fields and Biological Data", ed. Karl H. Pribram, 1993.
I don't believe that energy storage per se is likely to be a primary
function of such mechanisms, because the amount of energy stored is
probably fairly small compared to the amount of energy expended to maintain
the coherent state.  The coherent state would be maintained by a continuous
metabolic "pumping" which I would guess would expend quite a bit more
energy than would be actually stored.

Most of the possibilities I have seen discussed have to do with information
storage within the field, and rapid, non-local information processing
(non-local relative to the molecular scale of the underlying mechanisms --
still perhaps within cell, but possibly also between cells).  As you
suggest, the processes could easily be coordinating many transmembrane
proteins at once, or coordinating many processes distributed throughout the
cytoplasm via the cytoskeleton.   From the standpoint of neurobiology,
these processes might be controlling the integration of dendritic input on
the post-synaptic side, and also fine control of pre-synaptic response to
action potentials.

Most of the work has been theoretical rather than experimental... from my
own experience, it is clear that it is extremely rare that an experimental
biologist has the background to understand these theories, and even rarer
that a physicist or mathematician has any knowledge of experimental
biology.  This is currently a *very* ripe and unexplored field for
precisely this reason.

>Another important question: are these effects observed in cells taken from
>diverse taxonomic branches?

I believe that the Raman scattering effect does show up in many different
types of living cells.  I don't know about the microwave experiments.

Nick Szabo:
>And a third question: how can we manipulate and improve upon these
>biological quantum coherence mechanisms, if they exist?
>...If those arguing for quantum coherence are serious
>about it, rather than just grinding another axe against AI, they should
>be taking out patents right about now.

Indeed, I would expect this to be possible.  The argument for quantum
coherence is *NOT* an argument against AI.  Penrose may argue against AI
(and actually I think he only argues against AI as implemented by Turing
machines), but he is a very minor voice among the people doing real work in
this field.  Regarding patents... therein lies another personal axe of my
own, which I'll save for another time.

POSSIBLE BLOW TO CRYOGENICS:

Extropians who place a strong value in the idea of cryogenic suspension may
want to pay careful attention to developments in this rapidly growing
field.  If it turns out that coherent quantum fields *are* responsible for
storing some critical state components of human consciousness, then a total
suspension of all metabolic activity throughout the brain might result in
the irrevocable loss of this information.  It could be that continuous
metabolic activity must be always maintained in order to preserve important
portions of our memories and personalities.  The obvious analogy is to the
RAM in a computer system -- the information stored in the hardware may not
be preserved in the event of a total loss of metabolic activity (a "power
failure").

(Please -- no flames for this last paragraph!  I'm just calling 'em as I
see 'em... all I'm saying is that right now this is at least possible!
Yes, even if coherent fields *are* shown to be important, a "cold-boot"
still might be possible... we just have no way of knowing right now).

Doug

