From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sat, 24 Sep 1994 19:05:32 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-461] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

Doug Cutrell: 
> POSSIBLE BLOW TO CRYOGENICS:

You mean cryonics.

> If it turns out that coherent quantum fields *are* responsible for
> storing some critical state components of human consciousness, then a total
> suspension of all metabolic activity throughout the brain might result in
> the irrevocable loss of this information. 

Hmm, I thought quantum coherence was a highly parallel processing 
mechanism, not an improved storage mechanism.  Quantum coherence computation
is quite vulnerable to erratic quantum state collapse  -- indeed the folks
working on the computer science and physics sides find such collapse
to be so vulnerable to errors that high levels of isolation from
other quantum systems are required.  Many consider such
isolation impractical even under the most pristine conditions.
This would seem to make an especially poor candidate for long-term 
memory storage.  Cryonicists don't care about short-term memories 
(who wants to remember the horrid moments of ischemia onset?).  
Continuity in long term memory and personality, such as occurs before 
to after sleep or anasthesia, is what is crucial.  My (admittedly
limited) understanding is that such quantum coherence states collapse 
in tiny fractions of seconds, not hours; for information to persist 
over hours would require another mechanism to store the information
between such collapses.

I've long considered DNA and protein sequences an ideally dense
and (especially DNA) stable candidate for long term memory storage.
Despite the existance of analogous "learning" by the immune
system, apparently no one has found a mechanism for this, and few
expect to find one.  Still, I find DNA and protein to be far superior 
and much more probable storage mechanism for long term memory than a 
highly fragile quantum coherence phenomenon.

