 Many people assume that computers will always have this limitation,
but if there is a serious attempt at proving this, it is well hidden.

>2.  Has there yet been a refutation of Searle's Chinese Room theory?

 The standard version of the Chinese Room has an intelligent algorithm
being run on hardware (the human) whose intelligence is irrelevent.
Searle's argument only makes sense if you get confused about what is
is that passes the Turing Test. If you say it is the human, then you
see a contradiction. If you ignore the human and credit the algorithm
which the human is mechanically following, then there is nothing
surprising about the result.

 Tipler has an entertaining refutation (_The Physics of Immortality_, p. 40):
   If ... the human brain can code 10^15 bits ... then since an average
  book codes about 10^6 bits ... it would require more than 100 million
  books to encode the human brain. It would take at least thirty five-story
  main university libraries to hold this many books. We know from experience
  that we can access any memory in our brain in about 100 seconds, so the
  hand simulation of a Turing Test-passing program would require a human to
  be able to take off the shelf, glance through and return to the shelf all
  of these 100 million books in 100 seconds. If each book weighs about a
  pound and on the average the book moves one yard ... the rate of energy
  consumption is 3*10^11 megawatts ... requires a power output equal to
  that of 300 million large nuclear power plants. As I said, a man can no
  more hand-simulate a Turing Test-passing program than he can jump to
  the moon.
  ...
  Searle has proposed using the entire population of India (800 million
  people) to hand-simulate the program. ... such a modification destroys
  Searle's argument, because it is obvious to everyone that the entire
  human species can "know" things which no individual human knows. For,
  example, no individual human being has sufficient knowledge to make
  an automobile. [i.e. can't mine and refine all the metals needed, etc]

